<pre class='metadata'>
Title: TOON-LD Specification
Shortname: toon-ld
Level: 1
Status: LD
ED: https://kushbisen.github.io/toon-ld/
Editor: Kush Bisen, https://github.com/kushbisen
Abstract: TOON-LD (Token-Oriented Object Notation for Linked Data) is a text serialization format designed to minimize token count while preserving the semantic richness of JSON-LD. It achieves 40-60% token reduction compared to equivalent JSON-LD representations, making it ideal for Large Language Model (LLM) context windows and bandwidth-constrained applications.
Markup Shorthands: markdown yes
Date: 2025-01-15
</pre>

<pre class='link-defaults'>
spec:infra; type:dfn; text:string
spec:rfc2119; type:dfn; text:must
</pre>

Introduction {#intro}
=====================

TOON-LD extends the TOON (Token-Oriented Object Notation) format to support Linked Data semantics. It provides a human-readable, token-efficient alternative to JSON-LD that maintains full round-trip compatibility.

Relationship to JSON-LD {#relationship-jsonld}
----------------------------------------------

TOON-LD is designed as a serialization format for JSON-LD data. Any valid JSON-LD document can be converted to TOON-LD and back without loss of information. TOON-LD preserves:

- All JSON-LD keywords (`@context`, `@id`, `@type`, `@graph`, etc.)
- Prefix definitions and URI compaction
- Nested structures and arrays
- Value nodes with language tags and datatype annotations

Notation {#notation}
--------------------

The key words "MUST", "MUST NOT", "REQUIRED", "SHALL", "SHALL NOT", "SHOULD", "SHOULD NOT", "RECOMMENDED", "MAY", and "OPTIONAL" in this document are to be interpreted as described in [[!RFC2119]].

Design Goals {#design-goals}
=============================

1. **Token Efficiency**: Minimize the number of tokens when processed by LLM tokenizers
2. **Human Readability**: Maintain readability without sacrificing compactness
3. **Round-Trip Fidelity**: Guarantee lossless conversion to/from JSON-LD
4. **Streaming Support**: Enable incremental parsing for large documents
5. **Simplicity**: Keep the grammar simple enough for straightforward implementation

Lexical Structure {#lexical-structure}
=======================================

Character Set {#character-set}
------------------------------

TOON-LD documents MUST be encoded in UTF-8.

Line Termination {#line-termination}
------------------------------------

Lines are terminated by:
- Line Feed (U+000A) - LF
- Carriage Return followed by Line Feed (U+000D U+000A) - CRLF

Implementations MUST accept both forms and SHOULD produce LF when serializing.

Comments {#comments}
--------------------

TOON-LD does not support comments. All content is significant.

Reserved Characters {#reserved-characters}
------------------------------------------

The following characters have special meaning:

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Character</th>
      <th>Name</th>
      <th>Usage</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>:</code></td>
      <td>Colon</td>
      <td>Key-value separator</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>,</code></td>
      <td>Comma</td>
      <td>Value separator in arrays/rows</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>[</code> <code>]</code></td>
      <td>Brackets</td>
      <td>Array length notation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>{</code> <code>}</code></td>
      <td>Braces</td>
      <td>Tabular field list</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>@</code></td>
      <td>At sign</td>
      <td>JSON-LD keyword prefix</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>"</code></td>
      <td>Double quote</td>
      <td>String delimiter</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>^</code></td>
      <td>Caret</td>
      <td>Datatype annotation (<code>^^</code>)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>|</code></td>
      <td>Pipe</td>
      <td>Reserved for future use</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

Grammar {#grammar}
==================

EBNF Grammar {#ebnf-grammar}
----------------------------

<pre class='code'>
document        = object | array

object          = { property }

property        = key ":" value NEWLINE
                | key ":" NEWLINE indented-value
                | array-property

key             = jsonld-keyword | prefixed-name | string

jsonld-keyword  = "@" identifier

prefixed-name   = identifier [ ":" identifier ]

identifier      = ( ALPHA | "_" ) { ALPHA | DIGIT | "_" }

value           = primitive | inline-array | value-node

primitive       = null | boolean | number | string

null            = "null"

boolean         = "true" | "false"

number          = [ "-" ] digits [ "." digits ] [ exponent ]

digits          = DIGIT { DIGIT }

exponent        = ( "e" | "E" ) [ "+" | "-" ] digits

string          = unquoted-string | quoted-string

unquoted-string = safe-char { safe-char }

quoted-string   = '"' { string-char } '"'

safe-char       = (any character except ':', ',', '|', '"', leading/trailing whitespace)

string-char     = (any character except unescaped '"') | escape-sequence

escape-sequence = "\" ( '"' | "\" | "/" | "b" | "f" | "n" | "r" | "t" | unicode-escape )

unicode-escape  = "u" HEX HEX HEX HEX

inline-array    = "[" length "]:" [ value-list ]

value-list      = value { "," value }

array-property  = tabular-array | primitive-array

tabular-array   = key "[" length "]{" field-list "}:" NEWLINE { row }

primitive-array = key "[" length "]:" [ value-list ] NEWLINE
                | key "[" length "]:" NEWLINE { indented-value }

field-list      = field { "," field }

field           = key

row             = INDENT value-list NEWLINE

length          = digits

indented-value  = INDENT ( object | value NEWLINE )

value-node      = quoted-string "@" language-tag
                | quoted-string "^^" datatype

language-tag    = identifier [ "-" identifier ]

datatype        = prefixed-name | iri

iri             = "<" { iri-char } ">"

INDENT          = "  "  (exactly 2 spaces per nesting level)

NEWLINE         = LF | CRLF

ALPHA           = [A-Za-z]

DIGIT           = [0-9]

HEX             = [0-9A-Fa-f]
</pre>

Data Types {#data-types}
=========================

Null {#null}
------------

The literal `null` represents the absence of a value.

<div class='example'>
```
@context:
  schema: http://schema.org/
schema:middleName: null
```

Equivalent JSON-LD:

```json
{
  "@context": {
    "schema": "http://schema.org/"
  },
  "schema:middleName": null
}
```
</div>

Boolean {#boolean}
------------------

Boolean values are represented by the literals `true` and `false`.

<div class='example'>
```
@context:
  schema: http://schema.org/
schema:isAvailable: true
schema:isDeleted: false
```

Equivalent JSON-LD:

```json
{
  "@context": {
    "schema": "http://schema.org/"
  },
  "schema:isAvailable": true,
  "schema:isDeleted": false
}
```
</div>

Numbers {#numbers}
------------------

Numbers follow JSON number syntax:
- Optional leading minus sign
- Integer part
- Optional fractional part (`.` followed by digits)
- Optional exponent (`e` or `E`, optional sign, digits)

<div class='example'>
```
@context:
  schema: http://schema.org/
schema:numberOfItems: 42
schema:price: 19.99
schema:coefficient: 1.23e-4
```

Equivalent JSON-LD:

```json
{
  "@context": {
    "schema": "http://schema.org/"
  },
  "schema:numberOfItems": 42,
  "schema:price": 19.99,
  "schema:coefficient": 1.23e-4
}
```
</div>

Strings {#strings}
------------------

Strings can be quoted or unquoted. Unquoted strings MUST NOT:
- Contain `:`, `,`, `|`, or `"`
- Start or end with whitespace
- Be a reserved keyword (`null`, `true`, `false`)

Quoted strings support standard JSON escape sequences.

<div class='example'>
```
@context:
  foaf: http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/
foaf:name: Alice
foaf:mbox: "alice@example.org"
foaf:description: "A developer, researcher, and open data advocate"
foaf:workplaceHomepage: "C:\\Users\\Alice\\workspace"
```

Equivalent JSON-LD:

```json
{
  "@context": {
    "foaf": "http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/"
  },
  "foaf:name": "Alice",
  "foaf:mbox": "alice@example.org",
  "foaf:description": "A developer, researcher, and open data advocate",
  "foaf:workplaceHomepage": "C:\\Users\\Alice\\workspace"
}
```
</div>

Objects {#objects}
==================

Simple Objects {#simple-objects}
--------------------------------

Objects are represented as key-value pairs, one per line, with values following the colon separator.

<div class='example'>
```
@context:
  foaf: http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/
  schema: http://schema.org/
@type: foaf:Person
foaf:name: Alice
schema:age: 30
foaf:mbox: alice@example.org
```

This is equivalent to the following JSON-LD:

```json
{
  "@context": {
    "foaf": "http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/",
    "schema": "http://schema.org/"
  },
  "@type": "foaf:Person",
  "foaf:name": "Alice",
  "schema:age": 30,
  "foaf:mbox": "alice@example.org"
}
```
</div>

Nested Objects {#nested-objects}
--------------------------------

Nested objects are indented by 2 spaces per level.

<div class='example'>
```
@context:
  foaf: http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/
  schema: http://schema.org/
  vcard: http://www.w3.org/2006/vcard/ns#
foaf:Person:
  foaf:name: Alice
  vcard:hasAddress:
    vcard:street-address: 123 Main St
    vcard:locality: Springfield
    vcard:country-name: USA
```

Equivalent JSON-LD:

```json
{
  "@context": {
    "foaf": "http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/",
    "schema": "http://schema.org/",
    "vcard": "http://www.w3.org/2006/vcard/ns#"
  },
  "foaf:Person": {
    "foaf:name": "Alice",
    "vcard:hasAddress": {
      "vcard:street-address": "123 Main St",
      "vcard:locality": "Springfield",
      "vcard:country-name": "USA"
    }
  }
}
```
</div>

Arrays {#arrays}
================

Primitive Arrays {#primitive-arrays}
------------------------------------

Arrays of primitive values use the syntax `key[N]:` where `N` is the array length.

<div class='example'>
Inline format for short arrays:

```
@context:
  schema: http://schema.org/
schema:keywords[3]: RDF, "Linked Data", Semantics
```

Multi-line format for longer arrays:

```
@context:
  schema: http://schema.org/
schema:keywords[3]:
  RDF
  "Linked Data"
  Semantics
```

Both are equivalent to:

```json
{
  "@context": {
    "schema": "http://schema.org/"
  },
  "schema:keywords": ["RDF", "Linked Data", "Semantics"]
}
```
</div>

Empty Arrays {#empty-arrays}
----------------------------

Empty arrays are represented as `key[0]:` with no values.

<div class='example'>
```
@context:
  schema: http://schema.org/
schema:contributors[0]:
```

Equivalent to:

```json
{
  "@context": {
    "schema": "http://schema.org/"
  },
  "schema:contributors": []
}
```
</div>

Mixed Arrays {#mixed-arrays}
----------------------------

Arrays containing mixed types (objects, primitives, etc.) SHOULD use tabular format when objects are present, or indented format otherwise.

Tabular Arrays {#tabular-arrays}
=================================

Syntax {#tabular-syntax}
------------------------

Tabular arrays represent arrays of objects using CSV-like rows. The syntax is:

```
key[N]{field1,field2,...}:
  val1, val2, ...
  val3, val4, ...
```

Where:
- `N` is the number of objects in the array
- `{field1,field2,...}` lists all fields that appear in any object (the union)
- Each subsequent indented line is a row of values

Field Union Behavior {#field-union}
-----------------------------------

The field list in the header is the union of all keys across all objects. If an object is missing a field, `null` is inserted in that position.

<div class='example'>
```
@context:
  foaf: http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/
  schema: http://schema.org/
  vcard: http://www.w3.org/2006/vcard/ns#
foaf:knows[3]{foaf:name,schema:age,vcard:locality}:
  Alice, 30, null
  Bob, null, Portland
  Carol, 28, Seattle
```

Equivalent JSON-LD:

```json
{
  "@context": {
    "foaf": "http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/",
    "schema": "http://schema.org/",
    "vcard": "http://www.w3.org/2006/vcard/ns#"
  },
  "foaf:knows": [
    {"foaf:name": "Alice", "schema:age": 30},
    {"foaf:name": "Bob", "vcard:locality": "Portland"},
    {"foaf:name": "Carol", "schema:age": 28, "vcard:locality": "Seattle"}
  ]
}
```
</div>

Field Ordering {#field-ordering}
--------------------------------

Fields SHOULD be ordered alphabetically in the header for consistency and deterministic serialization.

Nested Values in Tabular Rows {#nested-tabular-values}
------------------------------------------------------

Tabular rows SHOULD only contain primitive values. If an object in the array has nested objects or arrays, the implementation MAY either:
1. Serialize the nested value as inline JSON
2. Fall back to non-tabular array representation

Quoting in Tabular Rows {#tabular-quoting}
------------------------------------------

Values in tabular rows follow CSV quoting rules:
- Values containing `,` MUST be quoted
- Quotes within quoted values are escaped by doubling: `""`

JSON-LD Keywords {#jsonld-keywords}
===================================

Core Keywords {#core-keywords}
------------------------------

TOON-LD supports all JSON-LD 1.1 keywords:

- `@context` - Prefix definitions
- `@id` - Resource identifier
- `@type` - Type declaration
- `@graph` - Named graph
- `@value` - Explicit value node
- `@language` - Language tag
- `@list` - Ordered list
- `@set` - Explicit set
- `@reverse` - Reverse properties
- `@base` - Base IRI
- `@vocab` - Default vocabulary

Additional Keywords {#additional-keywords}
------------------------------------------

JSON-LD 1.1 extended keywords are also supported:

- `@version` - JSON-LD version
- `@direction` - Text direction
- `@container` - Container type
- `@index` - Index property
- `@included` - Included nodes
- `@nest` - Nested properties
- `@prefix` - Prefix flag
- `@propagate` - Context propagation
- `@protected` - Protected term
- `@import` - Import external context
- `@json` - JSON literal type
- `@none` - Default index value

Value Nodes {#value-nodes}
--------------------------

Value nodes combine `@value` with `@language` or `@type` using compact syntax:

<div class='example'>
Language-tagged string:

```
@context:
  dc: http://purl.org/dc/terms/
dc:title:
  "Bonjour le monde"@fr
```

Typed literal:

```
@context:
  schema: http://schema.org/
  xsd: http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#
schema:birthDate:
  "1990-01-15"^^xsd:date
```

Equivalent JSON-LD:

```json
{
  "@context": {
    "dc": "http://purl.org/dc/terms/",
    "schema": "http://schema.org/",
    "xsd": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#"
  },
  "dc:title": {
    "@value": "Bonjour le monde",
    "@language": "fr"
  },
  "schema:birthDate": {
    "@value": "1990-01-15",
    "@type": "xsd:date"
  }
}
```
</div>

Keyword Ordering {#keyword-ordering}
------------------------------------

When serializing, JSON-LD keywords SHOULD be ordered as follows:

1. `@context` (always first if present)
2. `@id`
3. `@type`
4. Other `@` keywords
5. Regular properties

Context and URI Handling {#context-uri-handling}
=================================================

Context Serialization {#context-serialization}
----------------------------------------------

The `@context` is serialized as a nested object at the beginning of the document.

<div class='example'>
```
@context:
  foaf: http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/
  schema: http://schema.org/
  dc: http://purl.org/dc/terms/
  @base: http://example.org/
```

Equivalent JSON-LD:

```json
{
  "@context": {
    "foaf": "http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/",
    "schema": "http://schema.org/",
    "dc": "http://purl.org/dc/terms/",
    "@base": "http://example.org/"
  }
}
```
</div>

URI Compaction {#uri-compaction}
--------------------------------

When a context is present, full URIs SHOULD be compacted using defined prefixes.

<div class='example'>
```
@context:
  foaf: http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/
@type: foaf:Person
foaf:name: Alice
foaf:knows:
  foaf:name: Bob
```

Instead of using full URIs:

```
@context:
  foaf: http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/
@type: "http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/Person"
"http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/name": Alice
"http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/knows":
  "http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/name": Bob
```
</div>

URI Expansion {#uri-expansion}
------------------------------

When parsing TOON-LD with a `@context`, parsers MUST expand prefixed names to full URIs according to JSON-LD expansion rules.

Quoting and Escaping {#quoting-escaping}
=========================================

When to Quote {#when-to-quote}
------------------------------

A string value MUST be quoted if it:

1. Contains any of: `:` `,` `|` `"`
2. Starts or ends with whitespace
3. Is empty
4. Equals `null`, `true`, or `false`
5. Could be parsed as a number

Escape Sequences {#escape-sequences}
------------------------------------

Within quoted strings, the following escape sequences are recognized:

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Sequence</th>
      <th>Character</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>\"</code></td><td>Double quote (U+0022)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>\\</code></td><td>Backslash (U+005C)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>\/</code></td><td>Forward slash (U+002F)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>\b</code></td><td>Backspace (U+0008)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>\f</code></td><td>Form feed (U+000C)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>\n</code></td><td>Newline (U+000A)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>\r</code></td><td>Carriage return (U+000D)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>\t</code></td><td>Tab (U+0009)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>\uXXXX</code></td><td>Unicode code point</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

CSV Cell Escaping {#csv-cell-escaping}
--------------------------------------

In tabular array rows:
- Values containing commas MUST be quoted
- Embedded double quotes MUST be escaped by doubling (`""`)

<div class='example'>
```
@context:
  schema: http://schema.org/
schema:comments[2]{schema:author,schema:text}:
  Alice, "Said ""Hello, World!"""
  Bob, "Uses comma, semicolon; and quotes"
```

Equivalent JSON-LD:

```json
{
  "@context": {
    "schema": "http://schema.org/"
  },
  "schema:comments": [
    {"schema:author": "Alice", "schema:text": "Said \"Hello, World!\""},
    {"schema:author": "Bob", "schema:text": "Uses comma, semicolon; and quotes"}
  ]
}
```
</div>

Whitespace and Indentation {#whitespace-indentation}
=====================================================

Indentation {#indentation}
--------------------------

- Indentation MUST use exactly 2 spaces per nesting level
- Tabs MUST NOT be used for indentation
- Mixed tabs and spaces MUST NOT be used

Trailing Whitespace {#trailing-whitespace}
------------------------------------------

- Lines SHOULD NOT have trailing whitespace
- Parsers MUST tolerate trailing whitespace

Blank Lines {#blank-lines}
--------------------------

- Blank lines between properties are allowed but not required
- Parsers MUST skip blank lines
- Serializers SHOULD NOT emit blank lines

Parsing Algorithm {#parsing-algorithm}
=======================================

Parser States {#parser-states}
------------------------------

The parser operates in one of two modes:

1. **Indented Mode**: Default mode for parsing key-value pairs and nested objects
2. **CSV Mode**: Activated when a tabular array header is encountered

Indented Mode Parsing {#indented-mode-parsing}
----------------------------------------------

1. Read a line
2. Calculate indentation level (number of leading spaces / 2)
3. If line matches tabular header pattern `key[N]{fields}:`:
    - Switch to CSV mode
    - Parse N rows as CSV data
    - Return to indented mode
4. If line matches primitive array pattern `key[N]: values`:
    - Parse inline values as array
5. If line matches key-value pattern `key: value`:
    - If value is empty, parse nested content at deeper indent
    - Otherwise, parse value as primitive
6. Handle indentation changes for object nesting

CSV Mode Parsing {#csv-mode-parsing}
------------------------------------

1. Read the header to extract field names
2. For each of N rows:
    - Read line and trim indentation
    - Parse comma-separated values
    - Handle quoted values (CSV escaping rules)
    - Map values to field names
    - Create object from field-value pairs
3. Return array of objects
4. Return to indented mode

Value Parsing {#value-parsing}
------------------------------

1. If value is "null": return null
2. If value is "true": return true
3. If value is "false": return false
4. If value matches number pattern: return number
5. If value starts with '"': parse quoted string
6. If value contains '@' (language tag): parse value node
7. If value contains '^^' (datatype): parse typed literal
8. Otherwise: return unquoted string

Serialization Algorithm {#serialization-algorithm}
===================================================

Top-Level Serialization {#top-level-serialization}
--------------------------------------------------

1. If value is object:
    - Extract @context if present, build prefix map
    - Serialize @context first
    - Serialize remaining properties in keyword order
2. If value is array:
    - If array of uniform objects: use tabular format
    - If array of primitives: use inline/multiline format
    - Otherwise: use mixed array format
3. If value is primitive: serialize directly

Object Serialization {#object-serialization}
--------------------------------------------

1. Sort keys by keyword order (see [[#keyword-ordering]])
2. For each key-value pair:
    - Compact key using context prefixes
    - If value is object: serialize nested with increased indent
    - If value is array: choose appropriate array format
    - If value is primitive: serialize inline

Tabular Array Serialization {#tabular-array-serialization}
----------------------------------------------------------

1. Collect union of all keys from all objects
2. Sort fields (JSON-LD keywords first, then alphabetically)
3. Emit header: `key[N]{field1,field2,...}:`
4. For each object:
    - For each field in header order:
        - Get value or null if missing
        - Convert to CSV cell (quote if needed)
    - Emit row with proper indentation

Conformance {#conformance}
===========================

Conformance Levels {#conformance-levels}
----------------------------------------

**Level 1 - Basic:**
- Parse and serialize primitive values
- Parse and serialize simple objects
- Parse and serialize primitive arrays

**Level 2 - Standard:**
- All Level 1 features
- Parse and serialize tabular arrays
- Handle union-of-keys for non-uniform objects
- Support all JSON-LD core keywords

**Level 3 - Full:**
- All Level 2 features
- Support all JSON-LD 1.1 keywords
- URI compaction/expansion with @context
- Value node compact notation

Round-Trip Requirements {#round-trip}
-------------------------------------

A conforming implementation MUST satisfy:

```
parse(serialize(json_ld)) ≡ json_ld
serialize(parse(toon_ld)) ≡ toon_ld (modulo whitespace normalization)
```

Where `≡` indicates semantic equivalence (identical data, possibly different formatting).

Examples {#examples}
====================

Simple Document {#simple-document}
----------------------------------

<div class='example'>
JSON-LD:

```json
{
  "@context": {
    "foaf": "http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/"
  },
  "@id": "http://example.org/alice",
  "@type": "foaf:Person",
  "foaf:name": "Alice",
  "foaf:age": 30
}
```

TOON-LD:

```
@context:
  foaf: http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/
@id: "http://example.org/alice"
@type: foaf:Person
foaf:age: 30
foaf:name: Alice
```
</div>

Graph with Tabular Data {#graph-tabular-data}
---------------------------------------------

<div class='example'>
JSON-LD:

```json
{
  "@context": {
    "foaf": "http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/",
    "schema": "http://schema.org/"
  },
  "@graph": [
    {"@id": "ex:1", "@type": "foaf:Person", "foaf:name": "Alice", "schema:age": 30},
    {"@id": "ex:2", "@type": "foaf:Person", "foaf:name": "Bob", "schema:age": 25},
    {"@id": "ex:3", "@type": "foaf:Person", "foaf:name": "Carol", "schema:age": 35}
  ]
}
```

TOON-LD:

```
@context:
  foaf: http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/
  schema: http://schema.org/
@graph[3]{@id,@type,foaf:name,schema:age}:
  ex:1, foaf:Person, Alice, 30
  ex:2, foaf:Person, Bob, 25
  ex:3, foaf:Person, Carol, 35
```
</div>

Value Nodes {#value-nodes-example}
----------------------------------

<div class='example'>
JSON-LD:

```json
{
  "@context": {
    "xsd": "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#",
    "schema": "http://schema.org/"
  },
  "schema:name": [
    {"@value": "The Hobbit", "@language": "en"},
    {"@value": "Der Hobbit", "@language": "de"}
  ],
  "schema:datePublished": {
    "@value": "1937-09-21",
    "@type": "xsd:date"
  }
}
```

TOON-LD:

```
@context:
  xsd: http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#
  schema: http://schema.org/
schema:name[2]:
  "The Hobbit"@en
  "Der Hobbit"@de
schema:datePublished:
  "1937-09-21"^^xsd:date
```
</div>

Reverse Properties {#reverse-properties-example}
------------------------------------------------

<div class='example'>
JSON-LD:

```json
{
  "@context": {"schema": "http://schema.org/"},
  "@id": "http://example.org/book/1",
  "@type": "schema:Book",
  "schema:name": "The Great Novel",
  "@reverse": {
    "schema:about": [
      {"@id": "http://example.org/review/1"},
      {"@id": "http://example.org/review/2"}
    ]
  }
}
```

TOON-LD:

```
@context:
  schema: http://schema.org/
@id: "http://example.org/book/1"
@type: schema:Book
schema:name: The Great Novel
@reverse:
  schema:about[2]: "http://example.org/review/1", "http://example.org/review/2"
```
</div>

Security Considerations {#security}
====================================

Input Validation {#input-validation}
------------------------------------

Implementations MUST:
- Validate UTF-8 encoding
- Reject malformed escape sequences
- Limit recursion depth to prevent stack overflow (RECOMMENDED: 100 levels)
- Limit document size to prevent memory exhaustion
- Validate that array lengths match actual element counts
- Sanitize user input when generating TOON-LD dynamically

URI Handling {#uri-security}
----------------------------

When expanding or compacting URIs:
- Validate URI syntax before dereferencing
- Be cautious of URI schemes that may trigger actions (e.g., `javascript:`)
- Consider restricting allowed URI schemes
- Implement timeouts for remote context fetching
- Consider security implications of `@base` and relative URIs

Resource Limits {#resource-limits}
----------------------------------

Implementations SHOULD allow configuration of:
- Maximum document size
- Maximum nesting depth
- Maximum number of fields in tabular arrays
- Maximum line length
- Maximum string length
- Maximum array length

IANA Considerations {#iana}
============================

Media Type {#media-type}
------------------------

The proposed media type for TOON-LD is:

```
Type name: application
Subtype name: toon-ld
Required parameters: none
Optional parameters: charset (default: utf-8)
Encoding considerations: UTF-8
Security considerations: See Section 17
```

File Extension {#file-extension}
--------------------------------

The recommended file extension is `.toon` or `.toonld`.

Comparison with JSON-LD {#comparison}
======================================

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Aspect</th>
      <th>JSON-LD</th>
      <th>TOON-LD</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Delimiters</td>
      <td><code>{}</code>, <code>[]</code>, <code>:</code>, <code>,</code></td>
      <td><code>:</code>, <code>,</code> (minimal)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Key quoting</td>
      <td>Always quoted</td>
      <td>Never quoted</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>String quoting</td>
      <td>Always quoted</td>
      <td>Only when necessary</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Repetition</td>
      <td>Keys repeated per object</td>
      <td>Keys in header only</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Whitespace</td>
      <td>Insignificant</td>
      <td>Significant (indentation)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Typical token reduction</td>
      <td>Baseline</td>
      <td>40-60% fewer tokens</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

Token Savings Analysis {#token-savings}
========================================

For an array of N objects with K keys and average value length V:

**JSON-LD tokens (approximate):**
```
2 + N × (2 + K × (2 + 1 + 1)) = 2 + N × (2 + 4K) = 2 + 2N + 4NK
```

**TOON-LD tokens (approximate):**
```
1 + K + N × K = 1 + K + NK
```

**Savings ratio:**
```
(2 + 2N + 4NK - 1 - K - NK) / (2 + 2N + 4NK)
= (1 + 2N + 3NK - K) / (2 + 2N + 4NK)
≈ 3NK / 4NK = 75% for large N and K
```

In practice, savings of 40-60% are typical due to value token overhead.

Reference Implementation {#reference-implementation}
====================================================

The reference implementation is available at:

- **Repository:** https://github.com/argahsuknesib/toon-ld
- **Rust crate:** `toon-core`
- **npm package:** `toon-ld`
- **PyPI package:** `toon-ld`

Changelog {#changelog}
======================

Version 3.0 (Current) {#version-3}
----------------------------------

- Added union-of-keys behavior for tabular arrays
- Added support for all JSON-LD 1.1 keywords
- Added compact value node notation (`@lang`, `^^type`)
- Formalized quoting and escaping rules
- Added streaming parsing considerations

Version 2.0 {#version-2}
------------------------

- Added JSON-LD keyword support
- Added URI compaction/expansion
- Added tabular array format

Version 1.0 {#version-1}
------------------------

- Initial specification
- Basic TOON format with indentation-based nesting

